  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Carlos Rod\u00f3n lasted 4.1 innings, and the Yankees never recovered from the third inning when Milwaukee scored three straight runs to take the lead in a one-run loss.",
    "what_this_means": "The pattern is becoming harder to ignore. They scored first, held the lead through three innings, then watched it slip away in a single inning and never got it back. This is not new territory. They've been here twice in the last three games. But the margin for error keeps shrinking. With two runners in scoring position, they converted once. With ten strikeouts and six hits, the offense looked overmatched when it mattered most. Judge hit a solo shot for show, but the rest of the lineup produced noise without consequence. Milwaukee's third-inning flare was the game. Everything after was trailing and hoping, not leading and controlling. You don't lose games like this by accident. You lose them when your best pitcher can't go five.",
